{"id":"r9","level":1,"levelName":"Reading","n":9,"of":9,"title":"Where the weight falls","titleHe":"הַטְעָמָה","goal":"Put the stress in the right place — the last thing between you and a readable word.","sections":[{"type":"text","body":"You can now sound out any vocalized word. What's left is which syllable to lean on, and Hebrew is unusually tidy about it: the stress is on the last syllable unless the word is one of a few shapes where it isn't."},{"type":"table","caption":"Where the weight goes","head":["word","syllables","stressed"],"rows":[["שָׁלוֹם — hello","sha·lom","the last — shaLOM"],["תּוֹדָה — thanks","to·da","the last — toDA"],["מַתָּנָה — a gift","ma·ta·na","the last — mataNA"],["סֵפֶר — a book","se·fer","the first — SEfer"],["יֶלֶד — a child","ye·led","the first — YEled"],["בֹּקֶר — morning","bo·ker","the first — BOker"]]},{"type":"text","body":"The two names for this are worth knowing because grammar books use them and nothing else: מִלְּרַע is stress on the last syllable, מִלְעֵיל on the one before."},{"type":"tip","body":"The commonest מִלְעֵיל shape is the one in the last three rows — two syllables, the weight at the front, and a segol in the second: סֵפֶר, יֶלֶד, כֶּלֶב, דֶּגֶל. Grammarians call them segolates after that vowel. Once you can hear the shape, most of the exceptions are covered."},{"type":"tip","body":"Stress can be the whole difference between two words. בָּנוּ with the weight at the front is in us; בָּנוּ with the weight at the back is they built. Same letters, same marks — only the lean changes."},{"type":"text","body":"That finishes reading. From here the course is words and grammar, and every new word arrives with a transliteration so you can check the stress against your ear."}],"vocab":[{"he":"רֶגַע","en":"a moment","translit":"rega"},{"he":"דֶּגֶל","en":"flag","translit":"degel"},{"he":"חֶבֶל","en":"rope","translit":"chevel"},{"he":"כַּדּוּר","en":"ball","translit":"kadur"},{"he":"מַתָּנָה","en":"gift","translit":"matana"},{"he":"פִּתְאוֹם","en":"suddenly","translit":"pit'om"}],"exercises":[{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["a moment","story","flag","now"],"correct":0,"prompt":"רֶגַע","translit":"rega"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["evening","flag","invites, orders","tree"],"correct":1,"prompt":"דֶּגֶל","translit":"degel"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["apart from","rope","sheep, lamb","childhood"],"correct":1,"prompt":"חֶבֶל","translit":"chevel"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["seems, looks","decides","enters","ball"],"correct":3,"prompt":"כַּדּוּר","translit":"kadur"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["thirsty","gift","an emissary","chicken, fowl"],"correct":1,"prompt":"מַתָּנָה","translit":"matana"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["money, silver","teacher (f.)","suddenly","suitcase"],"correct":2,"prompt":"פִּתְאוֹם","translit":"pit'om"},{"type":"choice","q":"Where the weight goes — a gift?","options":["the first","the first","the first","the last"],"correct":3,"he":false},{"type":"read","he":"רֶגַע","translit":"rega","en":"a moment"},{"type":"listen","q":"Which word did you hear?","options":["חֶבֶל","כַּדּוּר","דֶּגֶל","פִּתְאוֹם"],"correct":2,"say":"דֶּגֶל","he":true},{"type":"choice","q":"How do you say “flag”?","options":["חֲנוּת","תֵּכֶף","דֶּגֶל","סֶלַע"],"correct":2,"he":true},{"type":"match","pairs":[["רֶגַע","a moment"],["דֶּגֶל","flag"],["חֶבֶל","rope"],["כַּדּוּר","ball"],["מַתָּנָה","gift"]]},{"type":"choice","q":"How do you say “suddenly”?","options":["נוֹלַד","אֵלֶּה","פִּתְאוֹם","סֻכָּר"],"correct":2,"he":true},{"type":"read","he":"דֶּגֶל","translit":"degel","en":"flag"},{"type":"choice","q":"Where the weight goes — thanks?","options":["the last","the first","the first","the first"],"correct":0,"he":false},{"type":"choice","q":"Where the weight goes — hello?","options":["the first","the first","the first","the last"],"correct":3,"he":false},{"type":"type","q":"Write “suddenly” in Hebrew","answer":"פִּתְאוֹם","hint":"pit'om"}]}